Course.start({"num":22,"unit":5,"slug":"m22-substitution","minutes":40,"calculator":"not needed","title":"Systems of Equations: Substitution","subtitle":"Solving a system exactly by replacing one variable with an expression","lede":"Graphing is great for seeing a solution, but it's hard to read (2/3, −5/3) off a grid. Substitution finds the exact answer with algebra.","recap":"A system's solution is the point that makes every equation true: where the graphs intersect. Parallel lines: no solution. Same line: infinitely many.","goals":["Solve a system by substitution when a variable is already alone","Isolate a variable first, then substitute","Write and solve systems for word problems"],"context":{"title":"Pheasants and rabbits","place":"China · about AD 400","text":"A Chinese math book called the ***Sunzi Suanjing*** (*The Mathematical Classic of Master Sun*) was written around **1,600 years ago**. It contains a puzzle that Chinese students still learn today:\n\n> \"Pheasants and rabbits are in the same cage. There are **35 heads** on top and **94 feet** below. How many pheasants and how many rabbits?\"\n\nEach animal has one head. A pheasant has 2 feet and a rabbit has 4. With {{p}} pheasants and {{r}} rabbits:\n\n> {{p + r = 35}}    and    {{2p + 4r = 94}}\n\nMaster Sun solved it with a clever trick: imagine every animal lifts half its feet. Then pheasants stand on 1 foot and rabbits on 2, for 47 feet in all. Each animal accounts for 1 of those feet, plus 1 more for each rabbit, so there must be 47 − 35 = **12 rabbits**, and **23 pheasants**.\n\nYou'll solve it this week with a method that works for every system: **substitution**.","ask":"Check Master Sun's answer: do 23 pheasants and 12 rabbits really have 35 heads and 94 feet?"},"learn":[{"title":"Substitution when a variable is alone","text":"If one equation already says {{y = …}} (or {{x = …}}), you can **replace** that variable in the other equation with the expression. Then you have **one equation with one variable**, which you know how to solve.","worked":[{"problem":"{{y = 2x}} and {{x + y = 9}}","steps":[["{{x + 2x = 9}}","Replace y with 2x in the second equation."],["{{3x = 9}}, so {{x = 3}}","Solve for x."],["{{y = 2(3) = 6}}","Put x = 3 back into either equation to find y."],["Solution: {{(3, 6)}}"]],"check":"3 + 6 = 9 ✓ and 6 = 2(3) ✓","eq":"y = 2x; x + y = 9","answer":"(3, 6)"}],"tip":"Use parentheses when you substitute an expression: replacing y with {{x - 4}} in {{3x - 2y = 10}} gives {{3x - 2(x - 4) = 10}}."},{"title":"Isolate first","text":"If no variable is alone, pick the **easiest** one to isolate: usually one with a coefficient of 1 or −1. Solve for it, then substitute.","worked":[{"problem":"{{x + 4y = 14}} and {{3x - 2y = 0}}","steps":[["{{x = 14 - 4y}}","x has coefficient 1 in the first equation: isolate it."],["{{3(14 - 4y) - 2y = 0}}","Substitute into the second, with parentheses."],["{{42 - 12y - 2y = 0}}, so {{42 = 14y}}, {{y = 3}}"],["{{x = 14 - 4(3) = 2}}","Back-substitute."],["Solution: {{(2, 3)}}"]],"eq":"x + 4y = 14; 3x - 2y = 0","answer":"(2, 3)"}],"check":{"q":"In {{2x + y = 7}} and {{5x - 3y = 1}}, which is easiest to isolate?","choices":[["{{y}} in the first equation",true,"Its coefficient is 1: y = 7 − 2x."],["{{x}} in the second equation",false,"You'd have to divide by 5 and get fractions."],["{{y}} in the second equation",false]]}},{"title":"Special cases and word problems","text":"If **both variables cancel** when you substitute:\n- a **false** statement (like {{3 = 7}}) means **no solution** (parallel lines);\n- a **true** statement (like {{4 = 4}}) means **infinitely many** solutions (the same line).\n\nFor a **word problem**: define two variables, write **two equations** (one for each fact), and solve.","worked":[{"problem":"Pheasants and rabbits: {{p + r = 35}}, {{2p + 4r = 94}}","steps":[["{{p = 35 - r}}","Isolate p."],["{{2(35 - r) + 4r = 94}}","Substitute."],["{{70 - 2r + 4r = 94}}, so {{2r = 24}}, {{r = 12}}"],["{{p = 35 - 12 = 23}}","23 pheasants, 12 rabbits."]],"eq":"p + r = 35; 2p + 4r = 94","answer":"(23, 12)"}]}],"tasks":[{"id":"t1","type":"steps","title":"Put the steps in order","intro":"Solving {{y = x - 1}} and {{2x + y = 11}}:","items":["{{2x + (x - 1) = 11}}","{{3x - 1 = 11}}","{{x = 4}}","{{y = 4 - 1 = 3}}","Solution: {{(4, 3)}}"],"hints":["Substitute first, then solve for x, then find y."]},{"id":"t2","type":"solve","title":"Substitute and solve","pick":4,"intro":"Type the solution as an ordered pair, like **(3, 6)**.","items":[{"q":"{{y = 3x}} and {{x + y = 16}}","a":"(4, 12)","eq":"y = 3x; x + y = 16","how":["{{x + 3x = 16}}, {{x = 4}}","{{y = 12}}"]},{"q":"{{y = x + 2}} and {{2x + y = 14}}","a":"(4, 6)","eq":"y = x + 2; 2x + y = 14","how":["{{2x + (x + 2) = 14}}, {{3x = 12}}, {{x = 4}}","{{y = 6}}"]},{"q":"{{x = 2y}} and {{x - 3y = -4}}","a":"(8, 4)","eq":"x = 2y; x - 3y = -4","how":["{{2y - 3y = -4}}, {{-y = -4}}, {{y = 4}}","{{x = 8}}"]},{"q":"{{y = -2x + 1}} and {{3x - y = 9}}","a":"(2, -3)","eq":"y = -2x + 1; 3x - y = 9","how":["{{3x - (-2x + 1) = 9}}, {{5x - 1 = 9}}, {{x = 2}}","{{y = -3}}"]},{"q":"{{y = 4x - 5}} and {{y = x + 1}}","a":"(2, 3)","eq":"y = 4x - 5; y = x + 1","how":["{{4x - 5 = x + 1}}, {{3x = 6}}, {{x = 2}}","{{y = 3}}"]},{"q":"{{x = y - 3}} and {{2x + 5y = 15}}","a":"(0, 3)","eq":"x = y - 3; 2x + 5y = 15","how":["{{2(y - 3) + 5y = 15}}, {{7y = 21}}, {{y = 3}}","{{x = 0}}"]}],"hints":["Replace the variable that's alone with its expression, in parentheses.","After you find one variable, plug it back in to find the other."]},{"id":"t3","type":"solve","title":"Isolate, then substitute","pick":4,"items":[{"q":"{{x + y = 10}} and {{x - y = 4}}","a":"(7, 3)","eq":"x + y = 10; x - y = 4","how":["{{x = 10 - y}}","{{(10 - y) - y = 4}}, {{y = 3}}, {{x = 7}}"]},{"q":"{{2x + y = 7}} and {{5x - 3y = 1}}","a":"(2, 3)","eq":"2x + y = 7; 5x - 3y = 1","how":["{{y = 7 - 2x}}","{{5x - 3(7 - 2x) = 1}}, {{11x = 22}}, {{x = 2}}, {{y = 3}}"]},{"q":"{{x - 2y = 1}} and {{3x + y = 10}}","a":"(3, 1)","eq":"x - 2y = 1; 3x + y = 10","how":["{{x = 1 + 2y}}","{{3(1 + 2y) + y = 10}}, {{7y = 7}}, {{y = 1}}, {{x = 3}}"]},{"q":"{{4x + y = -2}} and {{x + 2y = 3}}","a":"(-1, 2)","eq":"4x + y = -2; x + 2y = 3","how":["{{y = -2 - 4x}}","{{x + 2(-2 - 4x) = 3}}, {{-7x = 7}}, {{x = -1}}, {{y = 2}}"]},{"q":"{{3x - y = 4}} and {{6x - 2y = 8}}","a":"infinitely many","how":["{{y = 3x - 4}}","{{6x - 2(3x - 4) = 8}} becomes {{8 = 8}}: always true, **infinitely many**"]},{"q":"{{y - 2x = 1}} and {{4x - 2y = 6}}","a":"no solution","how":["{{y = 2x + 1}}","{{4x - 2(2x + 1) = 6}} becomes {{-2 = 6}}: false, **no solution**"]}],"hints":["Look for a variable with coefficient 1 or −1.","If the variables disappear: true statement → infinitely many; false → no solution."]},{"id":"t4","type":"mistake","title":"Substitution slips","intro":"Tap the **first wrong line**, then type the correct solution.","items":[{"q":"{{y = x - 4}} and {{3x - 2y = 10}}","lines":["{{3x - 2x - 4 = 10}}","{{x = 14}}"],"bad":0,"why":"The −2 must multiply the whole expression: {{3x - 2(x - 4) = 3x - 2x + 8 = 10}}, so {{x = 2}} and {{y = -2}}.","a":"(2, -2)","eq":"y = x - 4; 3x - 2y = 10"},{"q":"{{y = 2x}} and {{x + y = 12}}","lines":["{{x + 2x = 12}}","{{x = 4}}","Solution: {{x = 4}}"],"bad":2,"why":"You also need y: {{y = 2(4) = 8}}. The solution is the ordered pair {{(4, 8)}}.","a":"(4, 8)","eq":"y = 2x; x + y = 12"},{"q":"{{x + y = 5}} and {{2x + 3y = 12}}","lines":["{{x = 5 - y}}","{{2(5 - y) + 3y = 12}}","{{10 - y = 12}}","{{y = -2}}"],"bad":2,"why":"{{-2y + 3y = y}}, so {{10 + y = 12}} and {{y = 2}}, {{x = 3}}.","a":"(3, 2)","eq":"x + y = 5; 2x + 3y = 12"}]},{"id":"t5","type":"solve","title":"Word problems","intro":"Define two variables, write two equations, and solve. Type the answer asked for.","items":[{"q":"Master Sun's cage: **35 heads**, **94 feet**. How many **rabbits**?","a":"12","post":"rabbits","how":["{{p + r = 35}}, {{2p + 4r = 94}}","{{p = 35 - r}}: {{70 + 2r = 94}}, **r = 12**"]},{"q":"A school play sold **200 tickets**: adults **$8**, students **$5**. It made **$1,240**. How many **adult** tickets?","a":"80","post":"adult tickets","how":["{{a + s = 200}}, {{8a + 5s = 1240}}","{{s = 200 - a}}: {{8a + 1000 - 5a = 1240}}, {{3a = 240}}, **a = 80**"]},{"q":"Two numbers add to **50**. One is **4 times** the other. What's the **larger** number?","a":"40","how":["{{x + y = 50}}, {{y = 4x}}","{{5x = 50}}, {{x = 10}}, {{y = 40}}"]},{"q":"A rectangle's perimeter is **46 cm**. Its length is **5 cm more** than its width. What's the **width**?","a":"9","post":"cm","how":["{{2l + 2w = 46}}, {{l = w + 5}}","{{2(w + 5) + 2w = 46}}, {{4w = 36}}, **w = 9**"]}],"hints":["Each fact in the problem gives one equation.","Look for a variable you can isolate easily."]},{"id":"t6","type":"order","title":"Build the system","intro":"Build the equation for each fact. Use **a** for adult tickets and **s** for student tickets.","items":[{"q":"200 tickets were sold in all.","a":"a + s = 200","alt":["s + a = 200"],"extra":["8a"]},{"q":"Adult tickets cost $8 and student tickets $5. The total was $1,240.","a":"8a + 5s = 1240","alt":["5s + 8a = 1240"],"extra":["13"]}]}],"build":{"heading":"Write your own heads-and-feet puzzle","prompt":"Make up a puzzle like Master Sun's, with two kinds of things: chickens and cows, bikes and cars, 2-point and 3-point baskets, $5 and $10 bills. Give the **total count** and a **second total** (legs, wheels, points, dollars). Write the **system**, **solve it by substitution**, and **check** your answer.","model":"A parking lot has 20 bikes and cars and 56 wheels. Let b be bikes and c be cars. Then b + c = 20 and 2b + 4c = 56. From the first equation, b = 20 - c. Substituting: 2(20 - c) + 4c = 56, so 40 + 2c = 56 and c = 8. Then b = 12. Check: 12 + 8 = 20 and 2(12) + 4(8) = 56.","placeholder":"A ___ has ___ … Let ___ be …","reqs":[{"t":"Two equations with two variables","re":"\\d*\\s*[a-z]\\s*\\+\\s*\\d*\\s*[a-z]\\s*=","min":2},{"t":"Substitute (show an expression in parentheses)","re":"\\d\\s*\\(\\s*\\d+\\s*[-−+]"},{"t":"Find both values (two answers like c = 8)","re":"\\b[a-z]\\s*=\\s*[-−]?\\d+\\b","min":2},{"t":"Check your answer (use the word *check*)","re":"\\bcheck"}]},"reflect":{"q":"Master Sun's \"lift half your feet\" trick and the substitution method both get the answer. Do you like having one reliable method or learning clever tricks? Why?","placeholder":"I prefer… because…"},"challenge":[{"id":"x1","type":"solve","title":"Fractions and decimals","items":[{"q":"{{y = 1/2 x + 1}} and {{3x - 4y = 2}}","a":"(6, 4)","eq":"y = x/2 + 1; 3x - 4y = 2","how":["{{3x - 4(1/2 x + 1) = 2}}","{{3x - 2x - 4 = 2}}, {{x = 6}}, {{y = 4}}"]},{"q":"{{0.5x + 0.2y = 4}} and {{x - y = 1}}","a":"(6, 5)","eq":"0.5x + 0.2y = 4; x - y = 1","how":["{{x = y + 1}}","{{0.5(y + 1) + 0.2y = 4}}, {{0.7y = 3.5}}, {{y = 5}}, {{x = 6}}"]},{"q":"{{x/3 + y/2 = 4}} and {{x - y = -3}}","a":"(3, 6)","eq":"x/3 + y/2 = 4; x - y = -3","how":["{{x = y - 3}}","{{(y - 3)/3 + y/2 = 4}}; multiply by 6: {{2y - 6 + 3y = 24}}, {{y = 6}}, {{x = 3}}"]}]},{"id":"x2","type":"solve","title":"Mixture puzzle","items":[{"q":"A coffee shop mixes beans worth **$10/lb** with beans worth **$16/lb** to make **30 lb** worth **$12/lb**. How many pounds of the **$10** beans? ({{a + b = 30}}, {{10a + 16b = 12(30)}})","a":"20","post":"lb","how":["{{b = 30 - a}}","{{10a + 480 - 16a = 360}}, {{-6a = -120}}, **a = 20**"]},{"q":"You have **25 coins**, all dimes and quarters, worth **$4.00**. How many **quarters**?","a":"10","post":"quarters","how":["{{d + q = 25}}, {{0.10d + 0.25q = 4.00}}","{{0.10(25 - q) + 0.25q = 4}}, {{2.5 + 0.15q = 4}}, **q = 10**"]}]}],"culture":"**Did you know?** The same pheasant-and-rabbit puzzle traveled to Japan, where it's called *tsurukame-zan*, \"crane and turtle arithmetic\" (cranes have 2 legs, turtles have 4). Japanese students still learn it in elementary school.","unitTitle":"Systems and Exponents","code":"NDkwMC1UTkFTQUVIUA==","star":"MjY4OC1TSUdBTQ==","total":30,"prev":"m21-systems-graphing.html","next":"m23-elimination.html"});
